  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\,\u2713$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977500/assets/891959642b0d055d852329c59be7541179dd5ae0c4182c2bec533261ce3a700d.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ also lies in this quadrant.",
